      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "To solve the problem, we need to find the dimensions of Mateen's rectangular backyard such that it can be walked either 25 times along the length or 10 times around the perimeter. We will then calculate the area of the backyard using these dimensions.\n\nLet's denote the length of the backyard by \\( l \\) meters and the width by \\( w \\) meters.\n\n1. **Walking the length 25 times:**\n   If Mateen walks the length 25 times, the distance walked in this case is \\( 25l \\) meters, which is equal to 1000 meters. Therefore, we have:\n   \\[\n   25l = 1000\n   \\]\n   Solving for \\( l \\):\n   \\[\n   l = \\frac{1000}{25} = 40 \\text{ meters}\n   \\]\n\n2. **Walking the perimeter 10 times:**\n   If Mateen walks the perimeter 10 times, the distance walked in this case is \\( 10 \\times 2w \\) meters, which is also equal to 1000 meters. Therefore, we have:\n   \\[\n   10 \\times 2w = 1000\n   \\]\n   Simplifying this equation:\n   \\[\n   20w = 1000\n   \\]\n   Solving for \\( w \\):\n   \\[\n   w = \\frac{1000}{20} = 50 \\text{ meters}\n   \\]\n\n3. **Calculating the area of the backyard:**\n   The area \\( A \\) of Mateen's backyard is given by the product of its length and width:\n   \\[\n   A = l \\times w = 40 \\times 50\n   \\]\n   Performing the multiplication:\n   \\[\n   A = 2000 \\text{ square meters}\n   \\]\n\nTherefore, the area of Mateen's backyard is \\(\\boxed{2000}\\) square meters.",
